WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.820
Today's lecture is going to be
on practical transform coding,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.220
building on what we have
looked into in the last couple

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:12.720
of lectures.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.570
And we're going to
end it with showing

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.460
a very basic audio compressor.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.730
So we are getting hands-on.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.200
Cool.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.550
Let's start with the recap.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.620
So last time, we
talked about this thumb

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.510
rule for lossy
compression, which is,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.730
for a given
distortion measure, we

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.820
want to allocate more bits to
component with higher variance.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.190
So that was really the
one of the key takeaways

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.900
from last lectures.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.960
And we looked into
this example of water

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.000
filling intuition
for Gaussian sources.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.470
And again, the intuition, the
maths doesn't exactly work out,

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.650
like the KKT conditions and
everything, but the intuition

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.210
actually is used in--

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.290
it's basically applicable for
all sorts of lossy compressors

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.760
which are out there practical.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.410
And so this is like one of the
key takeaways in some sense.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.560
And so, water filling
problem was just recall the

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.680
recalling the problem.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.920
We were compressing two
independent Gaussian sources

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.900
with different variances.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.960
And then, for particularly
squared error distortion.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.500
This is important, again.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.530
We saw that the
rate-distortion function

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.370
can be given by this guy.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.770
And we saw some
takeaways, again,

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:25.210
that you need to--
you would want

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.060
to allocate more bits to the
signal with higher variance.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.800
And so, last time
quiz question was also

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.090
on just this exact thing
with some specific numbers.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.090
So we considered a setting with
sigma 1, square 1, sigma 2,

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.020
square 3.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.230
And then, at D equals
to 1.5, we asked,

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.420
what are the optimal
values of D1, D2.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:51.550
And I hope this one is obvious
that since 1.5d is greater than

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:55.180
sigma one square, that means
our D1 would just be sigma one

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.220
square and D2 would be
D minus D1 in this case.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:07.260
So the answer is just,
yeah, the fourth one.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.419
Then the second question
was, at D equals

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.280
to 2, what is the optimal rate?

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.470
So at D equals to 2--

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.530
this is same as you can verify
sigma 1 square plus sigma 2

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.580
square by 2.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.370
So basically, you will
give distortion of sigma

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.560
1 square to the first
component sigma 2 square

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.140
to the first component,
and this would

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.440
imply the rate for
the first stream

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.960
is 0 and similarly for
the second stream is 0

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.890
and hence you just need 0
bits per source component.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.060
So again, this is like--

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.340
I'm not getting into
details because we literally

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.600
covered this in last class.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.680
And this is just
putting specific values.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.650
OK, and then this
one part was at least

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.680
interesting for this question,
which is which of the following

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.600
is correct.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.240
So OK, so for D below
the two variances

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.660
we saw in class that you
want to keep D1 equals to D2.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.110
This was a consequence of
the optimization problem KKT

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.520
solution, which we got.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.640
So first part is correct.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.520
Again, the second part now asks
for D below the two variances.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.470
What about the bit rate?

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:14.210
So if you think about it,
if D1 is equals to D2,

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:18.920
our bit rate is half log sigma
square by D for each component.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.210
So if D1 is equal to D2, then
that would imply my rate one

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:27.290
is actually less than
rate 2 because my sigma 1

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.670
is less than sigma 2.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:31.400
So this is something like if
you didn't follow last lecture,

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.200
we are equally distributing
the distortion,

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.700
but you are still
giving higher rate

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.280
to the component
with higher variance,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:43.590
even when you are working with
lower distortion than both

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.760
the sigmas.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.370
And so this is also correct.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.740
Finally, for D between
the two variances,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.660
we know that at least
one of the components

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:52.770
will have zero bit rate.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.410
We can't really
guarantee the second one

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.060
because D is greater than--

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.850
it's like literally the first
part, so third is correct.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.985
So this was really the answer.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:08.750
OK, any questions
on the quiz part?

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:12.360
Everyone is comfortable with
the water-filling intuition now?

00:04:16.039 --> 00:04:16.539
Sorry.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.030
OK, so let's continue then.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:28.270
So after the Gaussian
water filling intuition,

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.070
the next part we
started looking into

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:33.100
was this transform coding setup.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.950
And what we highlighted is
like transform coding basically

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.720
provides two main benefits.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.305
One, it allows you
to decorrelate X.

00:04:41.305 --> 00:04:42.880
Why do you want to decorrelate?

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.250
Because once your
sources are decorrelated,

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.590
you can then use simpler
quantization schemes

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:52.120
to basically get similar level
of rate-distortion trade-off.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.230
So that's good.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:55.120
You want lower complexity.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.930
And the second benefit was this
idea of energy compaction, which

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:00.493
is really, you can
have more energy

00:05:00.493 --> 00:05:01.660
in the first few components.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.820
Again, tying back to the
water-filling intuition,

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.240
you want to have components
with very different variances

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.150
so that you can be you can
selectively allocate bits

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.010
to each of these components.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.890
And so this is
really the intuition

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:18.490
that you want to allocate
more bits to the components

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.260
with higher energy.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:22.750
And as a reminder, this
was our block diagram.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:26.720
So you just-- you inserted
a transformation step

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.960
between your old lossy pipeline.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.020
And then, whatever
you recover, you

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.030
run the inverse
transform to recover back

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:37.130
the original symbols.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:42.100
OK, and finally,
we talked about KLT

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.930
in particular, the
Karhunen-Loeve Transform.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.880
OK, and we did some maths.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.090
We revised the linear
algebra part of it.

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:56.800
This is really the
main idea, which is--

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.210
so OK, if you forget
everything do remember this.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.320
So KLT is basically an
eigenvalue-based linear

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:02.860
transform.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.270
So it's linear.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:07.330
And we saw how you
can compute it.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:11.920
And you can use to basically get
decorrelated components of X.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.550
A transform was this U
transpose matrix, where

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:18.340
U was the matrix
of the eigenvectors

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:23.110
of your covariance
matrix of X. We worked

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.510
through we worked
through this formula

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.130
by just like basic
linear algebra.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.970
But in today's class, we'll
look into one concrete example

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:35.090
so that you feel even more
comfortable about this idea.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:39.170
But again, remember
this throughout.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:42.200
The main idea of doing
this transform coding

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.260
is you transform the data,
in this case, to a new basis,

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.000
so this is a linear
transform basis,

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:51.230
makes sense, where the
components are uncorrelated

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:52.700
and have different variances.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.740
This is really the
key thing which

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.240
you want from your transform.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.350
OK, so today, we'll look
into some more examples

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.770
we'll introduce you to some
more practical transforms, which

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:05.970
is the DCT.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:09.120
Finally, we'll look at a
very basic audio compressor

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.790
which you can build
using all the components.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:12.840
We have learned so far.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:20.400
OK, any questions so far in the
previous lecture before we get

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:20.900
started?

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:26.760
OK, I guess we are good.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:30.550
OK, so we are going to look
into one specific example.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.310
So consider you
have a source which

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:36.810
looks like this, where Xn
is rho times Xn minus 1

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.090
plus square root 1
minus rho square,

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.760
so this is just a constant,
and then IID normal

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.980
0, comma, sigma square.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:47.040
OK, and your X0 is normal
zero, comma, sigma square.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:51.120
We'll try to understand the
transform coding for this source

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:52.650
working with blocks of two.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.870
So just k equals to
2 in our setting.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:04.860
OK, this is an example of a
Gauss-Markov source if you know.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.830
It's called
Gauss-Markov because--

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.710
Gaussian because we are
working with Gaussian sources.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:13.050
And if you recall like
when Shubham talked

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.460
about Markovian properties.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:15.810
So this one has Markovian.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:19.410
Your symbol at Xn just
depends on the past symbol

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.930
and some added random noise.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:25.350
So it has that
Markovian property.

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:29.460
So what I'm showing here
below on the left, this,

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.789
is just some sample
generated from this source.

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:36.630
So what you do is like you
just generate these samples,

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.370
and then you collect
every two of them.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.200
And we are plotting
them as this 2D plot.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.450
So you can think of this as X0.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:43.919
This is X1.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:46.830
Each time, you generate two
samples and you plot it.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:50.580
OK, and now you can clearly see
that there is some correlation.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.160
The next sample would depend
on the previous sample,

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.490
depending on this
sort of value of rho.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.140
So clearly, you can--

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:02.440
once the previous
sample, decent amount

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.320
about what the next sample
is going to be essentially.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:09.010
So hopefully, we should be able
to decorrelate and do something

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:11.170
with this source better.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:13.210
OK, so let's get started.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:18.700
First, let's just work out the
distribution of this source.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:23.590
So my X0 is normal 0,
comma, sigma square.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:32.510
And then my Xn is
just rho Xn minus 1

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:43.980
plus 1 minus rho squared,
normal 0 sigma square.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:45.090
OK.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:49.290
So let's just work out a few
of these components, what

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.130
their distribution looks like.

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.850
So just spelling
this formula out,

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.990
so your X1 would be
rho X of 0 plus this.

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:06.720
OK, and sensor noise,
so this is like IID

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.870
compared to whatever
my previous X is, now

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:12.690
you can actually figure
out the distribution of X1.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.660
So this is just sum of
two independent Gaussians,

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:20.520
and as a reminder for them,
the resulting distribution

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:22.830
of addition is just
your means get added

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.350
and your variances get added.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.510
So this is just like--

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:31.200
remember your basic
Gaussian stuff?

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.010
So your X1 in this case
would just be normal.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:38.580
So my X0 is this.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.280
And so my X1 would
be-- my means would

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.000
be just addition of this
zero plus this zero,

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.750
and then my variances would be--
so I also have this constant.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.700
So it just gets squared up.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:11:00.480
So rho square plus 1 minus
rho square times sigma square.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.190
Let me just write it like this.

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:08.910
And so your X1 is really just
normal 0, comma, sigma squared.

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:14.730
OK, so now you understand why
I had this random square root

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.470
1 minus rho square
rho constant, just

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.600
to make our life simpler
for this analysis.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:26.460
OK, so your X1, again,
so writing this clearly--

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.030
sorry.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:33.860
OK, so your X0 is normal
0, comma, sigma square.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:38.780
Your X1, again, we worked out as
normal 0, comma, sigma square.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.420
And then it's just
an iterative process.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:45.140
You can basically show
that your Xn will also

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:47.180
be just a normal 0,
comma, sigma square.

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.210
So the first point you
see is that this process

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:56.210
is stationary distribution
for each individual symbol.

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:02.600
OK, everyone with me so far?

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:06.830
OK, so next, what we want to do
is we want to apply KLT to this

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.470
to see if we can
decorrelate the samples.

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:13.020
We looked at it, we
thought about it.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.020
So for that, the first
step is we really

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:18.992
need to compute the covariance
matrix because we first

00:12:18.992 --> 00:12:20.450
compute the covariance
matrix, then

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:22.910
we compute its eigenvectors,
and that's how you actually

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:24.890
generate the transform.

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:27.980
And so question for
you guys, So what

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:33.110
is the 2 by 2 covariance
matrix sigma for this?

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:35.690
And just a reminder,
if people have

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:38.150
forgotten what covariance
matrix looks like,

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:40.190
it's just the expectation.

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:42.920
It's a 2 by-- it's going
to be a 2 by 2 matrix

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:45.200
where you are just
taking this expectation.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:53.380
And one hint really here is
that your process is stationary.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:55.045
So Is don't really matter.

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:16.795
While you guys are working
let me also work it out here.

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:54.960
OK, so first very quick
step is we are calculating

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:56.220
the covariance matrix.

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:58.590
In this case, we know
that means are zero.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:00.960
So let's make our life simpler.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:04.200
Let's not worry about
these expected values.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:05.160
All of them are zero.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.230
We just worked out
the distribution.

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:09.480
And we know that the
process is stationary.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:12.960
So you can just write it
your covariance matrix

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:16.020
as diagonal terms
are just expected

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:19.732
value of the same element,
X0, X0 transpose here, X1,

00:14:19.732 --> 00:14:20.880
X1 transpose.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:24.390
And the non-diagonal
elements are just X1 with X0.

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:26.670
So that's the covariance matrix.

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:29.850
If this was diagonal,
then you would

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:33.185
know that there is no
covariance between components

00:14:33.185 --> 00:14:33.810
which are away.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:38.770
And these are really
just like components.

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:41.580
They don't have any correlation.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:44.490
So in this case, working out
the expected value of X0,

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:49.290
X0 transpose, we also know that
this is a stationary process.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.870
X0 has same distribution
as X1, so this is

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:53.730
going to be same as this guy.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:59.460
And this we already know
because my X0 is same

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:03.180
as X1 and both of them are
distributed normal 0, comma,

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:05.570
sigma square.

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:08.325
So this guy is just
going to be sigma square.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:17.840
OK, this is just like plain
definition of variance,

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:20.360
didn't do anything fancy.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:24.320
Similarly, we can compute,
again, by symmetry.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:27.800
These two terms are
going to be same.

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:29.990
Now, let's compute this, though.

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:34.250
So now, this is not obvious
because X depends on X0.

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.400
So we can't just separate
things out and write it.

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:39.890
So let's just-- but we
know what exactly X1 is.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:42.770
We have the exact
expression for X1, so just--

00:15:42.770 --> 00:15:44.390
let's spell it out and write it.

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:49.250
So remember my X1 was rho
times x0 plus square root 1

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:51.140
minus rho square
this normal term.

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:54.440
Correct.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:59.810
And then, I want expected value
of this thing multiplied by X0.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:03.665
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:16:03.665 --> 00:16:05.940
Yeah, OK.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:06.720
Correct.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:11.500
OK, so anybody
just looking at it

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:14.260
wants to shout out the answer?

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.030
And then I'll work it out also.

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:24.010
Rho times sigma squared.

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:27.170
OK, one of the students
answered rho times sigma square.

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:28.940
And that's exactly correct.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:33.498
And it's not that hard to see if
you have seen a lot of Gaussians

00:16:33.498 --> 00:16:34.040
in your life.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:36.320
If you haven't,
just see a few more.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.620
And trust me, this
is not really hard.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:43.010
So first, we'll use the
linearity of the expectation

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:45.140
and we'll segregate
out the terms.

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.540
So expectation is
a linear operator.

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.180
So you get expected
value of rho times X0

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:54.530
square plus expected value
of 1 minus rho square,

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:57.650
this normal variable times X0.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:06.400
Then this term, the first one
is just rho times expected value

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:11.680
of X0 square which is nothing
but rho times sigma square.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:15.369
And the second one, we know
that these two terms are IID.

00:17:15.369 --> 00:17:18.250
And again, it's a very basic
property of expectation.

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:20.710
Once you have these
IID terms, you

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:23.140
can just write it
down as expected value

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:25.690
of the first term,
which is this.

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:31.000
I'm just going to write, this is
the constant, k, times normal 0,

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.780
comma, sigma square, and
then expected value of X0.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.120
But we know both of
them have mean 0.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:44.290
So this term just goes to 0 and
your final value just turns out

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:47.890
to be rho times sigma squared.

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:51.790
OK, so your diagonal
terms are just

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:56.500
sigma square and your
nondiagonal terms,

00:17:56.500 --> 00:17:58.990
are just rho times sigma square.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:07.020
So this is just like we
are doing step by step.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:08.910
We are going to apply
KLT to this process.

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.327
We are working
through these steps

00:18:10.327 --> 00:18:11.685
for this one specific example.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:15.740
Cool.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:19.180
Everyone with me?

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:20.950
So in this case, it's just--

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:22.540
I'm just writing it down.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:24.335
You can collect the
sigma squared term.

00:18:24.335 --> 00:18:25.750
It doesn't really matter.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:29.245
And you get this one rho matrix.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:39.190
OK, everyone is with me?

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:40.810
So what's the next step now?

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:43.052
Once you have the
covariance matrix,

00:18:43.052 --> 00:18:44.260
what are we going to do next?

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:47.950
Anyone?

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:50.120
What's the next step for KLT?

00:18:50.120 --> 00:18:51.620
Eigenvector decomposition.

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:55.750
OK, so the students answered
eigenvector decomposition.

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.110
That's exactly right.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:18:58.780
We have this matrix sigma.

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:01.840
We have to figure out it's--

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:03.340
remember the KLT step.

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:08.710
We have to figure out its
eigenvalue decomposition

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:17.980
or this diagonal transform where
U is your eigenvector matrix,

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:22.060
and lambda was just the
diagonal matrix of eigenvalues.

00:19:30.910 --> 00:19:33.610
OK, so earlier, I thought
I'll ask you guys to do,

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:35.480
but this is not a
linear algebra class.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:36.820
So go home and work this out.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:42.160
It's not very hard to work
this out, but do it once--

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:44.590
yourself.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:47.320
So this is just a 2 by 2 matrix.

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:50.170
It's not that hard to
compute its eigenvalues.

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:52.900
You can even use
like numerical tools.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.240
But basically, what you can
show that, in this case,

00:19:55.240 --> 00:20:00.070
your eigenvalues turns out to be
1 plus rho times sigma square.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.290
And 1 minus rho
times sigma square.

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:04.660
So your eigenvalues
somehow came out

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:06.970
to be like spreading
out the variances

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:10.840
from these two in some sense.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:14.620
And then you can also work out
the corresponding eigenvectors

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:16.990
and the eigenvector
corresponding

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:18.790
to this first eigenvalue.

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:21.940
You can show that it basically
is such that it's 1, 1.

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:23.530
So sorry.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:25.900
Yeah, it's basically
putting equal weight

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:27.490
to the both the components.

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:32.460
And the one corresponding
to the second one is 1, -1.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:38.140
OK, so now, can
somebody tell me what

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:41.290
are my transformed
components based on this KLT?

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:46.030
So we found the matrix
U using this thing.

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:48.460
So we can figure out--
so we found a transform,

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:52.150
so now we can get the
transform components.

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:55.510
Does anybody want to say what
the transform components are

00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:56.320
going to be?

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:07.530
Remember-- OK, so if you are
still scratchy about your linear

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:08.040
algebra--

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:11.280
so in this case your U
matrix will basically

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:12.660
look like 1 by root 2.

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:15.390
I'm just taking this one
by root 2 common, 1, 1,

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.780
which is my eigenvector
1, and 1, -1.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:23.410
And if you recall our
transform was U transpose.

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:27.900
I'm just going to continue here.

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:33.120
So that's going to be 1 by
root 2, just taking transpose.

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:43.600
And then your Y
is just T times X.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:46.720
So now you can see what this
transform really is doing.

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:51.160
What are the two
components going to be?

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:01.430
Anyone?

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:02.814
Yeah, go for it.

00:22:02.814 --> 00:22:04.590
[INAUDIBLE]

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:05.370
Exactly.

00:22:05.370 --> 00:22:07.320
So your components
are going to be

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.830
some normalized versions of
the sums and the difference

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:13.080
because when you--

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:13.755
let me just--

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:19.740
OK, let's just go
to the next one.

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:21.150
Can you guys see this?

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:21.840
Yeah, OK.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:23.520
So again, just same thing.

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:26.610
Your transform matrix looks
like this, what we worked out

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.770
in the last slide.

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:31.650
And therefore, now your
transform components is just

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:34.770
T times X. So you just
multiply this with the X

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.480
and you can show that
it looks like this.

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:41.640
So if you see what we did is
in this transform components--

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:44.670
what is this linear transform
at the end of the day doing?

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:48.120
You are just basically taking
some normalized version, so

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:49.530
some constant here.

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:51.480
And your first
component is just going

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:53.760
to be the sum of the two
elements in your block.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:56.880
And the second component is just
the difference between them.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:01.710
And what basically KLT transform
does is that these are now--

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:05.620
we know that these are
decorrelated components.

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:07.450
Actually, I shouldn't
have given that away.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:10.510
But yeah, we'll see that it is--

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:12.940
this results in
correlated components.

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:17.640
So again, this is just--

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:20.190
I'm repeating stuff
from previous slide.

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.170
Nothing has changed,
same example.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.570
We know our transform is this.

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:27.540
And now what am I showing
is on this left side

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.960
is the same 2 by 2 plot,
just showing samples

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:32.490
from this particular
Gauss-Markov

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:34.590
source for some
particular value of rho.

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:39.150
And so this is X1, this is X2.

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:41.040
And what I'm
showing on the right

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:43.900
is the transformed
data samples in 2D.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:49.200
OK, so this is-- now I'm just
plotting Y1 and Y2 for this one.

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:57.202
And now you can see
that maybe not that not

00:23:57.202 --> 00:23:58.410
that clear with these scales.

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:03.750
So this is like note that
the X range is -4 to 4,

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:06.030
Y range is also -4 to 4.

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:08.880
But basically, it
does feel like this

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:13.945
has reduced the correlation
between these two components.

00:24:13.945 --> 00:24:16.070
But we can't say just
looking at this scatter plot.

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:17.570
They might still be correlated.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:19.820
So the way to check
it is really now

00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:23.380
we need to calculate the 2
by 2 covariance matrix of Y.

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:24.440
That's really the idea.

00:24:24.440 --> 00:24:26.720
That's what we set out to do.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:32.420
So can somebody work out the 2
by 2 covariance matrix for Y?

00:24:38.395 --> 00:24:40.020
Actually, I'm going
to give the answer,

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:42.090
and I'm going to
leave this as a to-do.

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:44.250
So why don't you guys
work this out at home?

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:48.660
It's exactly-- and
if you have any--

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:52.560
if you are not able to work out,
get us in the office or on Ed.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:55.620
But what you can
basically show is

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:57.840
that the 2 by 2
covariance matrix,

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.210
so I'm just calling
sigma Y now of Y,

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:01.800
will look something like this.

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:08.430
OK, so now the diagonal term for
this matrix contains 1 plus rho

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:11.250
and 1 minus rho sigma square.

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:13.755
And the non diagonal
components are all zero.

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:20.640
And what I'm showing here
is, again, the same plot.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:23.610
So this is-- let me just
write it down maybe.

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:28.480
This is X1 or whatever, X2, X1.

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:30.080
And similarly, this is Y1, Y2.

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:35.960
And what I really changed
in this plot was the scale.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:38.840
So now I'm not really plotting
it with the same scale.

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:42.110
So my Y-axis goes
from -0.4 to 0.4.

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:46.260
My x-axis goes from -4 to 4.

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:48.750
So now you can clearly see
what this transform did.

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:52.350
It of resulted in a points which
are in a circle, which basically

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:56.040
you can't really tell, looking
at Y1, what my next value of Y2

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:57.090
is going to be.

00:25:57.090 --> 00:26:00.360
And another thing it did was
it actually spread it out

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:02.880
in different scales.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.670
So if you're familiar
with linear algebra

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:08.280
or actually you can just
look at these terms,

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:12.300
your variance in the Y1
is just going to be--

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:18.010
so let me just write it
maybe your variance in Y1

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:21.370
is just going to be 1
plus rho sigma square.

00:26:21.370 --> 00:26:24.280
Similarly, your
variance in Y2 is going

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:26.050
to be 1 minus rho sigma square.

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:33.130
OK, so actually, this
transform did both the things

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:36.010
which we wanted our
transform coding to do.

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:38.260
So the first part
was to decorrelate.

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:42.490
It indeed decorrelated
our transform components.

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:45.700
And moreover, it also gave
us two components which

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:46.960
have very different variants.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.140
And if we again tap back to
our water-filling intuition

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:52.030
from Gaussian
sources, this means

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:53.680
I can be very
selective about how

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:56.530
I allocate bits or distortion
to these two components.

00:27:00.877 --> 00:27:03.460
So basically, what we did is
for this particular [INAUDIBLE],,

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:05.320
we went from the
beginning to the end,

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:08.230
showing like what is KLT doing.

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:10.030
And it is indeed
achieving the properties

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:11.875
we wanted a transform
coder to achieve.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:23.940
Yeah, I'm going to pause
here for a few seconds

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:25.950
to see if anyone
has any questions

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:27.300
on this particular example.

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:40.690
OK, so everyone
with me, they can

00:27:40.690 --> 00:27:44.440
understand what KLT is doing,
how it's achieving what

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:48.440
we set out for it to achieve?

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:49.790
Yeah.

00:27:49.790 --> 00:27:52.850
So now let's discuss the--

00:27:52.850 --> 00:27:55.490
I would say, the main
idea not main idea,

00:27:55.490 --> 00:28:00.170
the main design choices you
get in the transform coding.

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:02.510
So again, summarizing
what we really

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:09.420
did was you had some
source, so here, OK,

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:12.630
actually, it's like you
had some source symbol.

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:16.380
OK, now I'm writing
it instead of X1, X2.

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:20.160
So instead of
writing it as X1, X2.

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:23.580
Xn, like we have been writing
just so that it makes sense

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:26.520
with our previous example,
I'm writing a source symbol

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:30.810
as x1, 1 x1, 2 to indicate
like the two components which

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.187
we were working with,
similarly X2, 1, x2,

00:28:33.187 --> 00:28:35.970
2, so on and so forth.

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:38.670
OK, so you have two
n components here.

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:41.330
OK, so what we did.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:43.670
First of all, in this
particular example,

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:47.060
we chose that we are going to
work with a 2 by 2 transform.

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:48.860
So that was a design choice.

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:50.630
We decided that the--

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:52.760
so in this particular
case, it was obvious

00:28:52.760 --> 00:28:54.350
that we are going
to choose the 2 by 2

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:56.225
transform because we
were working with a very

00:28:56.225 --> 00:28:57.920
specific Gauss-Markov
process where

00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:02.150
we knew the dependence
only goes one step behind.

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:04.160
But in practice,
you don't really

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:08.930
know like how much behind the
correlation between these time

00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:12.260
series goes or whatever
symbols you are getting goes.

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:18.830
OK, so your first thing is you
decide like how many blocks,

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:23.210
so how many-- what's the block
size on which you are going

00:29:23.210 --> 00:29:27.030
to do your transform coding at.

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:30.450
So in this example, we
took each two symbol

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:33.990
and we transformed it
into some Y1, 1, Y1, 2.

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:35.400
So what were we doing?

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:39.150
We chose one
particular point here.

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:41.340
We chose one
particular point here.

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:44.730
And we transformed it into
some other point here.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:45.690
So we did a 2 by 2.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:48.110
And we did it for all
many different points.

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:49.020
I'm making this up.

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:51.120
I don't know if this goes
in the right direction.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:54.330
These are just random cartoons.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:57.900
But really like we decided
to do this 2 by 2 transform.

00:29:57.900 --> 00:30:00.630
We could have done 4
by 4, 8 by 8, 16 by 16.

00:30:00.630 --> 00:30:03.060
Really up to us.

00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:05.790
But so far we have
only done transform.

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:07.980
This just gives
us this transform.

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:10.530
But if you recall,
after the transform is

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:14.430
the original old pipeline, which
Saki talked about in theory,

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:18.000
where you encode, you recall
all the old lectures, first two

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.250
lectures on lossy, where
we did quantization.

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:22.080
We came up with the codebooks.

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:24.840
You actually have to
still do all that.

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:28.260
At the end of the day, you
have to transmit a bitstream.

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:33.450
So after you converted it into
these transform components,

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:37.690
you still need to
quantize and send it.

00:30:37.690 --> 00:30:39.520
And the way people
do this, really,

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:42.220
or if you look at the Gaussian
water filling example,

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:44.440
like the intuition
we got, is you

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:48.100
can think of each transform
component as a stream.

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:53.210
So like this purple block
is like a single stream.

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:55.250
And then the-- so in
this particular case,

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:59.180
since we were working with two
components, we have two streams.

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:02.060
And then for each stream,
now we can independently

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:04.370
apply whatever quantization
scheme we want,

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:07.520
scalar quantization,
vector quantization.

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:09.380
We can, again, have
different block sizes

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:12.930
for the vector quantization.

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:15.698
So really, another
thing which I want

00:31:15.698 --> 00:31:17.490
to highlight through
this example, and this

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:21.180
is a very common
transform coding

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.850
transform coding
lossy pipeline, just

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:33.070
first you transform [AUDIO OUT]
transform to [AUDIO OUT]

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:36.910
That gives you the
transform components.

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:41.520
But then, you independently
quantize each stream.

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:43.830
Why do you want to independently
quantize each stream?

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:45.663
That's where you actually
take the advantage

00:31:45.663 --> 00:31:48.630
that, oh, both my streams
have very different variances.

00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:50.970
Like for example, in
our previous case,

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:54.930
stream 1 variance-- so I'll just
write it like variance of stream

00:31:54.930 --> 00:32:03.500
1 components is going to be
this 1 plus rho sigma square.

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:08.500
Similarly, your variance
of stream two components

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:12.770
is going to be 1 minus
rho sigma square.

00:32:12.770 --> 00:32:15.080
So now I can use my
water-filling intuition

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:18.080
to allocate different number
of bits to both of these.

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:25.830
Everyone comfortable
with the idea?

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:26.370
OK.

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:29.640
So in practice that
basically means knob.

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:32.490
You immediately have two
knobs to play with whenever

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:35.310
you are given some
lossy compressor, which

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:37.410
is working in transform domain.

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:40.980
So maybe it's very easy to
see it through some example.

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:47.670
Let's say you are given two bits
per sample, and somebody says,

00:32:47.670 --> 00:32:50.700
oh, go do a lossy
compression on top of it.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.460
So first design decision,
which you have to take really

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:58.320
is what's my per channel
or per transform component

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:00.210
bit rate split?

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:02.760
So this is the
idea where we give

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:07.230
more bitrate to the
component with higher energy.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:08.460
So what are my options?

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:11.400
OK, maybe I could have
given 1 bit to stream 1,

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:13.530
1 bit to stream 2.

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:17.010
Maybe I could have given 2 bit
to stream 1, 0 bit to stream 2.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:20.500
I just say I'm never going to
transmit this because, again,

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:21.750
you can think of what feeling.

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:24.450
Maybe I don't need to
transmit this, or maybe like

00:33:24.450 --> 00:33:27.090
any such split between the two.

00:33:27.090 --> 00:33:29.910
It's a valid split.

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:33.120
OK, and then so--

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:37.505
maybe let's just write it
down, see some confused spaces.

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:58.860
So how many bits to
allocate to each bit stream?

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:00.480
So again, in our
previous example

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:04.620
where we want to encode
with one bit per symbol,

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:07.170
let's say I decide
that I'm going

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:09.690
to do 1, 1, which
is equal bit split,

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:12.810
or maybe I'm going to do 2, 0.

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:15.870
OK, this makes sense.

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:16.830
I'm just averaging.

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:19.199
So on average, in
the 2, 0, case I'm

00:34:19.199 --> 00:34:24.120
still using one bit per symbol,
the basic idea of block.

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:28.350
And then my step two,
is after I decide this,

00:34:28.350 --> 00:34:39.120
I can choose quantization for
each stream independently.

00:34:46.909 --> 00:34:49.670
OK, so let's say my--

00:34:49.670 --> 00:34:55.300
I decided with, let's say 1, 1.

00:34:55.300 --> 00:34:59.800
So then I can decide, OK, my
component 1 or stream one--

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:02.410
so I'm going to be using
these terms interchangeably.

00:35:02.410 --> 00:35:04.630
People in practice also
use them interchangeably.

00:35:04.630 --> 00:35:06.250
So if you are confused just ask.

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:09.610
Sometimes, it's called per
channel, per component,

00:35:09.610 --> 00:35:12.250
per stream, depending
on the context.

00:35:12.250 --> 00:35:15.130
But I hope you are able to
follow what's really going on.

00:35:15.130 --> 00:35:17.050
If you're not, just ask me.

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:20.530
OK, so maybe I decide
for component 1

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:24.940
I'm going to use a
scalar quantizer.

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:28.900
And I'm going to keep my-- so as
soon as I say scalar quantizer,

00:35:28.900 --> 00:35:30.850
and I know it's one
bits per sample,

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:32.860
I know my codebook size is 2.

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:41.720
So this is now going back
to our quantization lecture.

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:44.660
Maybe for component 2, though--

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:46.280
so OK, actually,
I won't do that.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:47.600
I'll show you exact example.

00:35:47.600 --> 00:35:50.000
This is just to give you
what all options you have.

00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:52.220
For the component
2, I can say no, I'm

00:35:52.220 --> 00:35:53.660
going to do vector quantizer.

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:01.770
And then your problem, in some
sense, it's like a recursion.

00:36:01.770 --> 00:36:04.830
It just goes back to what
we saw during quantization.

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:09.150
So now you can think
of this second stream

00:36:09.150 --> 00:36:11.340
as just independent symbols.

00:36:11.340 --> 00:36:13.540
And you can blocks out of it.

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:16.590
And you can create your
vector codebook from it.

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:20.190
You can actually compress
it in that fashion.

00:36:25.440 --> 00:36:27.390
The vector quantizer--
that's not sufficient.

00:36:27.390 --> 00:36:30.720
I also need to tell you what's
the sizes of my vectors, which

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:32.400
I'm going to work with.

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:35.700
Let's say I say 2.

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:40.680
So I'm going to work with
vectors of dimension 2.

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:45.060
So in this case, my codebook
size for second component,

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:49.500
let me also add a subscript,
maybe 1 for component 1.

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:51.942
And component 2 is just
going to be 2 raised to 2.

00:36:51.942 --> 00:36:53.400
So it's going to--
we're have we're

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:56.860
going to have four components.

00:36:56.860 --> 00:37:01.270
So really, in practice,
now you have got two knobs

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:04.540
to play as soon as you
do transform coding.

00:37:04.540 --> 00:37:07.510
First is you can decide,
OK, what kind of transform

00:37:07.510 --> 00:37:08.380
we want to do.

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:11.200
But after that, you can
decide at what block size do

00:37:11.200 --> 00:37:13.150
I have to do this transform?

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:14.980
Again, and this
is very important

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:16.480
because in practice
you don't really

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:18.820
know the covariance matrix.

00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:21.670
You don't know what's really the
correlation between the signals

00:37:21.670 --> 00:37:23.350
which you are going to get.

00:37:23.350 --> 00:37:26.650
And then the second
knob is the same knob,

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:28.867
which is like quantization,
which we have looked at.

00:37:28.867 --> 00:37:30.700
I can change the number
of codebook vectors.

00:37:30.700 --> 00:37:31.930
I can do vector quantization.

00:37:31.930 --> 00:37:35.380
I can do 10 other things.

00:37:35.380 --> 00:37:38.470
And this you will see in
all practical compressors.

00:37:38.470 --> 00:37:42.580
You can play with
these two things.

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:46.900
We'll see an example of it,
some version of example of it

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:48.940
when we get to the
audio coding part.

00:37:48.940 --> 00:37:53.920
But always remember-- so
when you are tuning for RD,

00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:56.020
you can change your
rate distortion

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:57.550
based on both of these.

00:37:57.550 --> 00:37:59.740
And what people do in
practice is actually

00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:02.500
they change both of these,
and they get these numbers,

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:05.650
and then they choose the best
one for a given [AUDIO OUT]

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:08.080
So for example, if--

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:10.990
again, we'll talk more about
it with one specific example.

00:38:10.990 --> 00:38:13.828
But let's say you're a streaming
service, in that case, what

00:38:13.828 --> 00:38:14.620
you're going to do?

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:17.410
You're going to actually play
with both of these knobs.

00:38:17.410 --> 00:38:22.582
You're going to get lots of
different compressed streams

00:38:22.582 --> 00:38:24.040
at different rates,
and then you're

00:38:24.040 --> 00:38:25.248
going to choose the best one.

00:38:25.248 --> 00:38:27.910
Sometimes, changing quantization
is going to help you more.

00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:30.820
Sometimes, changing the
block size of your transform

00:38:30.820 --> 00:38:35.000
maybe is going to help you
more so on and so forth.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:35.815
Yeah.

00:38:35.815 --> 00:38:39.710
In the example of each stream
