00:44:18.030 --> 00:44:18.720
experiment.

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:21.660
So we are not doing
any transform.

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:24.030
I'm fixing the bits
per symbol as one.

00:44:24.030 --> 00:44:25.740
So in all of these
experiments, it's

00:44:25.740 --> 00:44:29.490
the same idea from
what we just discussed.

00:44:29.490 --> 00:44:31.620
So my bits per symbol is one.

00:44:31.620 --> 00:44:36.060
OK, so the first knob which I
can play with is the block size.

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:37.830
So this is just doing
vector quantization,

00:44:37.830 --> 00:44:39.480
increasing block size.

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:42.870
All of these experiments
are at a fixed rate.

00:44:42.870 --> 00:44:45.960
So this makes sense.

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:48.630
And so obviously, as you
increase the block size,

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:50.850
your distortion decreases.

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:54.120
Not surprising, but your
complexity also increases.

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:56.640
So now you have to generate
codebook and four dimensions.

00:44:56.640 --> 00:44:59.760
You have to figure out
points in four dimension.

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:03.510
What the second block is showing
is a transform coding vector

00:45:03.510 --> 00:45:04.590
quantization experiment.

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:06.270
So this is for transform coding.

00:45:06.270 --> 00:45:08.460
So first, you
transform, and this

00:45:08.460 --> 00:45:10.463
is using the exact same
transform which we just

00:45:10.463 --> 00:45:11.505
derived for this example.

00:45:14.050 --> 00:45:17.080
And now, after you
have decided that-- so

00:45:17.080 --> 00:45:19.570
in all of these examples,
I'm fixing the block size

00:45:19.570 --> 00:45:20.470
of the transform.

00:45:20.470 --> 00:45:21.370
It's just two.

00:45:21.370 --> 00:45:24.520
Again, bits per
symbol is always 1.

00:45:24.520 --> 00:45:29.020
And then I can decide my
bitrate split bit components,

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:30.130
exactly what we discussed.

00:45:30.130 --> 00:45:33.340
So I can give equal
bitrates to each of them.

00:45:33.340 --> 00:45:36.580
I can give maybe more
bitrate to the second one

00:45:36.580 --> 00:45:38.620
or maybe more bitrate
to the first one.

00:45:38.620 --> 00:45:41.290
0 just means I throw away
that component entirely.

00:45:41.290 --> 00:45:42.715
I don't even care.

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:50.400
And so what do you
guys think looking

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:52.860
at-- so this is the-- again,
all of them have rate 1.

00:45:52.860 --> 00:45:56.280
This is the distortion.

00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:58.530
What do you think happened here?

00:46:07.040 --> 00:46:09.320
So I would say look
at this number.

00:46:09.320 --> 00:46:11.540
So this was 0.163.

00:46:11.540 --> 00:46:13.400
We are fixing block size 2.

00:46:13.400 --> 00:46:15.500
Everywhere we are
fixing block size each

00:46:15.500 --> 00:46:17.210
of the step for the
transform coding.

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:24.880
And 0.163, and for
different bit rate splits,

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:30.390
I see very different
levels of distortion.

00:46:30.390 --> 00:46:31.650
So what's happening?

00:46:36.550 --> 00:46:37.630
Not a trick question.

00:46:37.630 --> 00:46:38.860
Just say it out loud.

00:46:42.080 --> 00:46:44.210
First one has a
lot more variance,

00:46:44.210 --> 00:46:47.610
so they need more space
to get lower distortion.

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:50.590
If we put our bits
in the [AUDIO OUT]

00:46:50.590 --> 00:46:52.720
goes really high as we
see in the second one.

00:46:52.720 --> 00:46:53.290
Exactly.

00:46:53.290 --> 00:46:55.300
So this is now a
very concrete example

00:46:55.300 --> 00:46:56.740
of what we have been discussing.

00:46:56.740 --> 00:46:59.380
So what is this?

00:46:59.380 --> 00:47:02.080
What we see is,
oh, if I give more

00:47:02.080 --> 00:47:04.960
bitrate to the first component,
which had higher variance,

00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:10.690
remember it had 1 plus
rho sigma square variance

00:47:10.690 --> 00:47:14.020
whereas the second
one had 1 minus rho.

00:47:14.020 --> 00:47:16.300
In that case, I
get something which

00:47:16.300 --> 00:47:21.840
has much better
performance than this 0.163

00:47:21.840 --> 00:47:23.430
with equal complexity.

00:47:23.430 --> 00:47:25.710
I'm still-- so obviously,
OK not really equal

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:28.590
because I have to do the
transform step extra but equal

00:47:28.590 --> 00:47:31.260
quantization complexity.

00:47:31.260 --> 00:47:34.458
Whereas if I did exact
opposite, if I gave more bits

00:47:34.458 --> 00:47:36.000
to the symbol which
had lower energy,

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:37.830
like stream which
had lower energy,

00:47:37.830 --> 00:47:40.170
I suddenly pay a huge penalty.

00:47:40.170 --> 00:47:43.620
And clearly, equally
splitting which between them

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:45.240
is not the optimal thing to do.

00:47:49.080 --> 00:47:53.560
OK, everyone send me?

00:47:53.560 --> 00:47:58.850
OK, so now, in the next one,
I'm going to change the rho.

00:47:58.850 --> 00:48:03.060
And the question really
is, What do you expect?

00:48:03.060 --> 00:48:05.040
If I increase the
rho, do you expect

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:06.810
to see more benefits
or less benefits?

00:48:10.908 --> 00:48:12.280
More.

00:48:12.280 --> 00:48:13.690
OK, more.

00:48:13.690 --> 00:48:14.975
Let's have a show of hands.

00:48:14.975 --> 00:48:15.850
We can work this out.

00:48:15.850 --> 00:48:17.590
This is a binary choice.

00:48:17.590 --> 00:48:19.120
So I'm going to increase rho.

00:48:19.120 --> 00:48:21.100
So I'm going to increase
the correlation.

00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:22.930
And the question
which I'm asking is,

00:48:22.930 --> 00:48:25.630
What do you expect, like
the transform coding VQ

00:48:25.630 --> 00:48:29.350
when I give unequal bit rate
splits to the two components,

00:48:29.350 --> 00:48:31.585
do you expect more
advantages or less?

00:48:34.450 --> 00:48:36.325
How many of you say less?

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:42.970
How many of you say more?

00:48:42.970 --> 00:48:45.355
OK, at least half of the
class is neither more or less,

00:48:45.355 --> 00:48:48.055
so equal, I guess.

00:48:52.240 --> 00:48:56.860
OK, do expect more because
the more correlated

00:48:56.860 --> 00:48:59.380
the sources are--

00:48:59.380 --> 00:49:00.550
so there are two things.

00:49:00.550 --> 00:49:03.190
One, it's harder to get
good performance from them

00:49:03.190 --> 00:49:06.640
by using lower dimensional
vector quantizer.

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:11.020
But the really the main point
is as you change your rho,

00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:15.250
remember the variances were
1 plus rho sigma square

00:49:15.250 --> 00:49:17.830
and 1 minus rho sigma square.

00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:24.160
So as rho increases
my gap increases.

00:49:24.160 --> 00:49:27.920
So that's allowing me to do more
of this water filling stuff.

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:30.430
I want to have more and
more gaps so that I can just

00:49:30.430 --> 00:49:32.920
throw away the lower
variance component sooner

00:49:32.920 --> 00:49:36.700
rather than later, correct?

00:49:36.700 --> 00:49:40.930
And so here what I'm showing
is the same [AUDIO OUT] 0.99.

00:49:40.930 --> 00:49:45.920
And now here you can
see that this is 0.107,

00:49:45.920 --> 00:49:49.940
but now when I do bitrate
split between 2, 0,

00:49:49.940 --> 00:49:52.970
my distortion just
goes down to just 0.03

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:55.130
compared to 0.107 here.

00:49:55.130 --> 00:50:01.470
Whereas here, it just barely
beat it, 0.163 to 0.122.

00:50:01.470 --> 00:50:04.020
And also, you can see
how high this gap really

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:06.720
is like if you allocate
more bits to the component

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:09.480
with higher variance versus
lower variance or higher energy

00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:12.770
versus lower energy.

00:50:12.770 --> 00:50:14.720
OK, one point I
didn't say, but you

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:19.070
can see that even in this,
going to block size 4 still

00:50:19.070 --> 00:50:20.900
beats this.

00:50:20.900 --> 00:50:24.500
So again, vector-- so the
point is vector quantization.

00:50:24.500 --> 00:50:26.060
You shouldn't forget about it.

00:50:26.060 --> 00:50:27.567
It is really powerful.

00:50:27.567 --> 00:50:29.150
It's just it's very
compute-intensive.

00:50:29.150 --> 00:50:32.690
So you have to be selective
about when to throw rocket

00:50:32.690 --> 00:50:36.560
at your problem versus when you
can just get away with an arrow.

00:50:36.560 --> 00:50:38.600
So what transform coding
is allowing you to do

00:50:38.600 --> 00:50:44.310
is, at times, letting you get
away with an arrow really.

00:50:44.310 --> 00:50:45.960
And so this is like
same experiment,

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:48.510
but this time, I
reduce rho to 0.5.

00:50:48.510 --> 00:50:52.830
And actually, in this case, even
while giving the maximum bitrate

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:56.070
split, I'm not able to beat it.

00:50:56.070 --> 00:50:58.410
And actually, this, I'm going
to leave as a take-home.

00:50:58.410 --> 00:50:59.310
Think about it.

00:50:59.310 --> 00:51:02.760
Ask an officer hour or on Ed.

00:51:02.760 --> 00:51:07.560
But it's actually not surprising
that 2, 0 is not beating.

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:09.390
And I'll give you a hint.

00:51:09.390 --> 00:51:19.960
So the question really is why
transform coding doesn't beat--

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:23.830
so I'm not filling in the
variables, but the variables.

00:51:23.830 --> 00:51:31.830
--doesn't beat VQ for
rho equals to 0.5.

00:51:31.830 --> 00:51:33.810
And I'll give you a hint.

00:51:33.810 --> 00:51:41.990
Our gap, or sigma 1 is
1 plus rho sigma square.

00:51:41.990 --> 00:51:44.900
And sigma 2 is 1 minus
rho sigma square.

00:51:44.900 --> 00:51:48.860
And at this point, these
are [INAUDIBLE] IIDs.

00:51:48.860 --> 00:51:51.740
You actually know exactly
the solution to this problem.

00:51:51.740 --> 00:51:55.840
So given a total distortion
and a total rate,

00:51:55.840 --> 00:51:59.050
we have worked out the exact
solution to this problem.

00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:03.590
And so when rho is
decreasing, basically, you

00:52:03.590 --> 00:52:06.680
are probably in a scenario
where variance is not

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:08.990
in the middle of
the two variances.

00:52:08.990 --> 00:52:11.840
Maybe the total
distortion which is there

00:52:11.840 --> 00:52:13.730
is below the first
component also.

00:52:13.730 --> 00:52:16.790
So you can't throw away the
first component completely.

00:52:16.790 --> 00:52:19.910
So again, this is like a
repeat of water filling.

00:52:19.910 --> 00:52:21.590
So remember, in the
water-filling case,

00:52:21.590 --> 00:52:24.770
like we don't always throw
away the component with lower

00:52:24.770 --> 00:52:25.430
variance.

00:52:25.430 --> 00:52:27.230
There is a case
when your distortion

00:52:27.230 --> 00:52:28.640
is lower than the 2 sigma.

00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:31.880
You still want to keep, but
you will give it a lower rate.

00:52:31.880 --> 00:52:34.250
So in this
experiment, I'm just--

00:52:34.250 --> 00:52:36.380
I didn't find the correct split.

00:52:36.380 --> 00:52:38.060
That's really the intuition.

00:52:38.060 --> 00:52:41.330
You could have worked out
the exact bitrate split,

00:52:41.330 --> 00:52:45.110
and then that could
have beaten the thing.

00:52:45.110 --> 00:52:46.550
Go home, think more about it.

00:52:46.550 --> 00:52:48.322
Actually, try to
work this out exactly

00:52:48.322 --> 00:52:50.030
like the previous quiz
question, and that

00:52:50.030 --> 00:52:51.238
will give you some intuition.

00:52:54.558 --> 00:52:55.100
Any question?

00:53:03.680 --> 00:53:04.180
Cool.

00:53:04.180 --> 00:53:06.950
Everyone's with me?

00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:10.610
So I hope this example
gave you a better

00:53:10.610 --> 00:53:14.840
idea of what's really happening
with the transform coding.

00:53:14.840 --> 00:53:20.120
But now let's get into the
practical aspect of it.

00:53:20.120 --> 00:53:22.880
Nobody actually uses
KLT in practice,

00:53:22.880 --> 00:53:26.410
even though we did all this.

00:53:26.410 --> 00:53:29.500
We talked about it a
little bit, but now I--

00:53:29.500 --> 00:53:32.440
looking through this example,
I gave you a better intuition

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:33.460
why we don't do it.

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:36.340
So your KLT is basically
dependent on the statistics

00:53:36.340 --> 00:53:37.600
of input.

00:53:37.600 --> 00:53:40.820
Recall we had to--

00:53:40.820 --> 00:53:41.320
where is it?

00:53:41.320 --> 00:53:41.820
Where is it?

00:53:41.820 --> 00:53:42.460
Where is it?

00:53:45.560 --> 00:53:48.830
Yeah, they call what all
we did for this example.

00:53:48.830 --> 00:53:53.240
We first had to figure out a
distribution given the samples,

00:53:53.240 --> 00:53:54.180
ideally.

00:53:54.180 --> 00:53:57.020
Or we could have tried to
estimate the covariance matrix

00:53:57.020 --> 00:54:00.320
directly, which is a
function of the input data.

00:54:00.320 --> 00:54:03.590
What that means is I have to
recompute this every time I

00:54:03.590 --> 00:54:05.360
get a new input data.

00:54:05.360 --> 00:54:08.600
I have to I have to keep
redoing this computation.

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:11.600
And moreover, this computation
is not trivial in the sense

00:54:11.600 --> 00:54:14.390
that it costs you to do the
eigenvalue decomposition.

00:54:14.390 --> 00:54:17.540
We didn't really talk about the
complexity aspect of it here,

00:54:17.540 --> 00:54:21.080
but it costs you some compute
time to do all of this.

00:54:21.080 --> 00:54:22.430
So it's not really ideal.

00:54:22.430 --> 00:54:25.190
You don't want to add an extra
compute time at each step

00:54:25.190 --> 00:54:26.660
of doing the transform.

00:54:26.660 --> 00:54:34.900
And that's really one of the
main issues with the KLT.

00:54:34.900 --> 00:54:37.240
Another issue, which I think
one student pointed out

00:54:37.240 --> 00:54:41.170
last class is, in practice,
we don't even know sigma.

00:54:41.170 --> 00:54:42.730
So we have to estimate it.

00:54:42.730 --> 00:54:45.270
And there'll be some errors
also because of that.

00:54:45.270 --> 00:54:47.920
So I think that's still I would
say the secondary concern--

00:54:47.920 --> 00:54:50.290
the primary concern
really is it's

00:54:50.290 --> 00:54:53.550
computationally
intensive and slow,

00:54:53.550 --> 00:54:57.540
so we can't really
work this out.

00:54:57.540 --> 00:55:02.960
And so this leads to leads us
to the practical transforms.

00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:05.060
The question really
is, I don't want

00:55:05.060 --> 00:55:07.850
to keep recomputing my
matrix again and again,

00:55:07.850 --> 00:55:10.340
my matrix T for new data.

00:55:10.340 --> 00:55:14.030
So can I come up with some
structured transform, which

00:55:14.030 --> 00:55:17.060
is a way of saying, can I
come up with a fixed matrix

00:55:17.060 --> 00:55:21.650
so as to say T, which doesn't
change with the data, but just

00:55:21.650 --> 00:55:26.180
easy to compute, and
basically has good energy

00:55:26.180 --> 00:55:27.590
compaction properties.

00:55:27.590 --> 00:55:29.960
So if your matrix T
doesn't de-correlate,

00:55:29.960 --> 00:55:35.570
doesn't do a energy compaction,
then that's basically a useless

00:55:35.570 --> 00:55:36.110
transform.

00:55:36.110 --> 00:55:39.350
You won't be able to exploit it
for the compression part, which

00:55:39.350 --> 00:55:40.428
we saw later.

00:55:40.428 --> 00:55:42.470
Again, so in the previous
example, if you recall,

00:55:42.470 --> 00:55:45.800
one intuition is as my
correlation increased,

00:55:45.800 --> 00:55:48.500
I basically got two
components with a bigger gap

00:55:48.500 --> 00:55:50.360
in their variance,
and that allowed

00:55:50.360 --> 00:55:53.000
me to compress my
source really better.

00:55:53.000 --> 00:55:56.360
So ideally, I want this
energy compaction thing

00:55:56.360 --> 00:56:00.080
to happen for my
underlying compressor,

00:56:00.080 --> 00:56:03.350
for mandolin quantizer
to take benefit.

00:56:03.350 --> 00:56:06.860
And so we are going to
talk about something

00:56:06.860 --> 00:56:10.880
called DCT, discrete cosine
transform, a little bit.

00:56:10.880 --> 00:56:12.770
And it's amazing.

00:56:12.770 --> 00:56:16.575
It's basically used in so
many compressors out there.

00:56:16.575 --> 00:56:18.950
And if you are familiar with
Fourier transforms, discrete

00:56:18.950 --> 00:56:20.735
Fourier transforms,
and the like,

00:56:20.735 --> 00:56:24.920
you can think of it
as a sibling of DFT.

00:56:24.920 --> 00:56:27.860
Basically, it's another one
of the frequency transform.

00:56:27.860 --> 00:56:29.730
And this is really,
really funny.

00:56:29.730 --> 00:56:33.110
So Kedar, I think, found
out this was really nice.

00:56:33.110 --> 00:56:38.850
So he is the author who came
up with the DCT transform idea.

00:56:38.850 --> 00:56:40.320
And you can look
at this article.

00:56:40.320 --> 00:56:43.200
It's actually very fun
read, weekend fun read.

00:56:46.120 --> 00:56:50.500
And so these are two
excerpts from that article.

00:56:50.500 --> 00:56:53.110
And so if you look at the
first one, what Nasser says

00:56:53.110 --> 00:56:57.820
is what intrigued him
was the KLT was indeed

00:56:57.820 --> 00:56:58.820
the optimal transform.

00:56:58.820 --> 00:56:59.320
So we?

00:56:59.320 --> 00:57:02.380
Know KLT is optimal.

00:57:02.380 --> 00:57:05.020
And yet there is no
efficient algorithm

00:57:05.020 --> 00:57:07.090
available to compute it.

00:57:07.090 --> 00:57:11.050
So that's really the issue so
this guy said the same thing.

00:57:11.050 --> 00:57:13.457
And then, as such, the
focus of his research

00:57:13.457 --> 00:57:15.790
was to determine whether it
would be possible to come up

00:57:15.790 --> 00:57:17.680
with a good
approximation to the KLT,

00:57:17.680 --> 00:57:19.720
which can be computed
efficiently, which is

00:57:19.720 --> 00:57:21.760
essentially what we want to do.

00:57:21.760 --> 00:57:25.150
And he came up with this idea of
DCT, which is used everywhere.

00:57:25.150 --> 00:57:27.940
So clearly, it was
very successful in his

00:57:27.940 --> 00:57:29.290
in his endeavor.

00:57:29.290 --> 00:57:31.480
And then even more
funny, and this

00:57:31.480 --> 00:57:32.830
is like an anecdotal research.

00:57:32.830 --> 00:57:35.530
So if you are doing your
PhD or research, don't get

00:57:35.530 --> 00:57:38.140
demotivated if you
believe in your idea

00:57:38.140 --> 00:57:42.460
but nobody else does because
much to his disappointment,

00:57:42.460 --> 00:57:45.590
in his case, NSF didn't
even fund his proposal,

00:57:45.590 --> 00:57:47.930
fund their proposal.

00:57:47.930 --> 00:57:50.990
And again, like DCT
basically, as Shubham

00:57:50.990 --> 00:57:55.310
said whenever you are opening
some image or some gzipping,

00:57:55.310 --> 00:58:00.980
when you open any compressed
file on your computer, at least

00:58:00.980 --> 00:58:02.960
multimedia files,
most likely you

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:04.730
are running some
DCT transform on

00:58:04.730 --> 00:58:07.400
that every click, every
time you have an image

00:58:07.400 --> 00:58:08.840
loading on your browser.

00:58:08.840 --> 00:58:10.760
There is a decompression
step happening,

00:58:10.760 --> 00:58:14.600
and DCT is somewhere most
likely involved in that step.

00:58:14.600 --> 00:58:18.410
Whatever multimedia it is,
be it audio, video, YouTube,

00:58:18.410 --> 00:58:20.400
Netflix Spotify.

00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:24.410
It's everywhere.

00:58:24.410 --> 00:58:26.525
Yeah, it has clearly
made quite an impact.

00:58:30.290 --> 00:58:34.670
And so after this fun detour.

00:58:34.670 --> 00:58:39.800
Let's get back to this question
of the practical transforms.

00:58:39.800 --> 00:58:43.890
So we want to come up with
this structured transform.

00:58:43.890 --> 00:58:45.690
Oh, one second.

00:58:45.690 --> 00:58:48.672
OK, and in practice,
there are-- you

00:58:48.672 --> 00:58:50.630
will see a lot of options
for these transforms.

00:58:50.630 --> 00:58:53.520
So I just said discrete cosine
transform is just one of them.

00:58:53.520 --> 00:58:54.780
But you can think of discrete.

00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:56.550
So if you have done signal
processing in your life,

00:58:56.550 --> 00:58:58.842
at any point, you would have
heard some of these terms,

00:58:58.842 --> 00:59:01.230
discrete Fourier transform,
wavelet, so on and so forth.

00:59:01.230 --> 00:59:04.500
Actually, there is a very nice
list on Wikipedia for all the

00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:07.980
[INAUDIBLE],, I would say,
Fourier-based transform.

00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:09.540
It was nice to look at it.

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:11.873
A lot of people have spent
a lot of time and thinking

00:59:11.873 --> 00:59:13.290
of all these
different transforms,

00:59:13.290 --> 00:59:17.100
and they have very strong
mathematical properties.

00:59:17.100 --> 00:59:19.830
We are not going to redo
the signal processing part.

00:59:19.830 --> 00:59:22.200
I will just take some
time to quickly give you

00:59:22.200 --> 00:59:23.970
intuition of what
these are doing,

00:59:23.970 --> 00:59:27.630
and we'll refer you to more
signal processing literature

00:59:27.630 --> 00:59:28.800
if you want to learn more.

00:59:28.800 --> 00:59:30.840
So I want to say
up front that this

00:59:30.840 --> 00:59:33.840
is going to be a hand-wavy
discussion of DCT.

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:37.650
DCT is actually a very,
or all of these transforms

00:59:37.650 --> 00:59:40.050
are actually have very nice
mathematical properties.

00:59:40.050 --> 00:59:41.550
You can study them
very rigorously.

00:59:41.550 --> 00:59:44.240
And there are courses, whole
courses, which do that.

00:59:44.240 --> 00:59:47.390
So I'll encourage you guys
to take them if you like.

00:59:47.390 --> 00:59:49.010
But these are the
basic ideas which

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:51.470
are really important
for the compression when

00:59:51.470 --> 00:59:55.290
it comes to these transforms.

00:59:55.290 --> 00:59:57.510
OK, point one is most
of these transforms

00:59:57.510 --> 01:00:00.890
are based on the idea of the
orthogonal or orthonormal basis,

01:00:00.890 --> 01:00:01.890
which we have looked at.

01:00:01.890 --> 01:00:06.060
So we are still working
with the same idea.

01:00:06.060 --> 01:00:09.120
Many of them will exploit
the sparsity of signal

01:00:09.120 --> 01:00:10.470
in some basis.

01:00:10.470 --> 01:00:13.290
By some basis, I mean
some transformation.

01:00:13.290 --> 01:00:17.070
So for example, you will see
that DCT exploits the sparsity

01:00:17.070 --> 01:00:19.890
of the signal in cosine basis.

01:00:19.890 --> 01:00:22.920
What that means is if you
have some input symbols, X,

01:00:22.920 --> 01:00:24.630
you apply DCT to it.

01:00:24.630 --> 01:00:27.630
You get this DCT
frequency components.

01:00:27.630 --> 01:00:29.610
If it's a natural
signal, most likely,

01:00:29.610 --> 01:00:32.460
you will see that
you will try to have

01:00:32.460 --> 01:00:34.537
sparse transform components.

01:00:34.537 --> 01:00:36.120
So you will have
transform components,

01:00:36.120 --> 01:00:39.720
which are already zero, so that
allows you to throw them away.

01:00:39.720 --> 01:00:42.420
So they will have
very low energy.

01:00:42.420 --> 01:00:45.360
So that fits very well
with what we want to do.

01:00:45.360 --> 01:00:49.960
They give you the energy
compaction property.

01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:52.120
And the main idea
why these work--

01:00:52.120 --> 01:00:56.230
so obviously, OK,
Nasser said, OK, DCT

01:00:56.230 --> 01:00:59.800
was also a good approximation
to KLT under certain settings.

01:00:59.800 --> 01:01:01.570
We won't really talk
about that here.

01:01:01.570 --> 01:01:06.350
But one key takeaway
why these things works

01:01:06.350 --> 01:01:10.110
is because of the natural
signal statistics.

01:01:10.110 --> 01:01:11.790
What that means is
that if you look

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:16.230
at a natural image, a natural
audio, whatever image I'm

01:01:16.230 --> 01:01:18.540
seeing here, a natural
video, and then

01:01:18.540 --> 01:01:20.530
you do some frequency
transform of it

01:01:20.530 --> 01:01:23.190
and you try to see, OK, what
are the frequency components?

01:01:23.190 --> 01:01:26.880
We'll see that most of the
energy of these symbols

01:01:26.880 --> 01:01:31.610
will lie at a lower frequency
compared to higher frequency.

01:01:31.610 --> 01:01:35.360
So you have this
natural content,

01:01:35.360 --> 01:01:38.120
which basically doesn't have
a lot of energy in the higher

01:01:38.120 --> 01:01:39.380
frequency components.

01:01:39.380 --> 01:01:41.390
And then, using one
of these transforms,

01:01:41.390 --> 01:01:44.820
you can exploit
that essentially.

01:01:44.820 --> 01:01:48.150
OK, so this is really
one of the key ideas

01:01:48.150 --> 01:01:51.030
why this constant
transform works.

01:01:51.030 --> 01:01:55.530
It works because your signal
itself has statistics,

01:01:55.530 --> 01:01:59.100
and you can use this transform
to extract that property

01:01:59.100 --> 01:02:01.530
that, OK, it is sparse in
higher frequency component.

01:02:01.530 --> 01:02:04.140
So when I transform it,
I will get lower values

01:02:04.140 --> 01:02:07.280
of these transform components.

01:02:07.280 --> 01:02:12.350
OK, this is really, I would say,
in some sense, one key intuitive

01:02:12.350 --> 01:02:13.040
jump.

01:02:13.040 --> 01:02:14.090
It wasn't obvious.

01:02:14.090 --> 01:02:19.550
You need to know that your world
around you is low-pass filtered,

01:02:19.550 --> 01:02:20.750
so as to say.

01:02:23.720 --> 01:02:25.730
And actually, it's
just not this.

01:02:25.730 --> 01:02:31.790
So this gives you the Gaussian
water-filling kind of intuition

01:02:31.790 --> 01:02:33.980
of why these transforms work.

01:02:33.980 --> 01:02:37.220
But in reality, a lot
of these compressors,

01:02:37.220 --> 01:02:39.800
like when you actually
see how they throw away

01:02:39.800 --> 01:02:41.510
the bits for
different components

01:02:41.510 --> 01:02:44.450
or how they assign bits for
the different components.

01:02:44.450 --> 01:02:46.280
It's not just based on energy.

01:02:46.280 --> 01:02:49.940
It's also based on who is
consuming the final lossy

01:02:49.940 --> 01:02:52.130
compressed signal.

01:02:52.130 --> 01:02:54.710
So for example,
images or audio, these

01:02:54.710 --> 01:02:57.260
are to be consumed by humans
at the end of the day.

01:02:57.260 --> 01:02:59.540
And we as humans
also don't really

01:02:59.540 --> 01:03:01.160
see high-frequency signals.

01:03:01.160 --> 01:03:03.530
So if you have a lot of
high-frequency signal

01:03:03.530 --> 01:03:04.100
in an image.

01:03:04.100 --> 01:03:06.110
So if you have a lot
of noise, you just

01:03:06.110 --> 01:03:07.250
do a low-pass filter of it.

01:03:07.250 --> 01:03:10.040
You won't even see that noise if
it's extremely high frequency.

01:03:10.040 --> 01:03:14.750
Similarly, with audio, our
frequency range at which we hear

01:03:14.750 --> 01:03:17.210
is, say, 20hz to 20khz.

01:03:17.210 --> 01:03:20.600
But in reality, very few
humans actually go that wide.

01:03:20.600 --> 01:03:23.910
But for this discussion,
let's say 20hz to 20khz.

01:03:23.910 --> 01:03:26.670
So if you have a
signal and it has

01:03:26.670 --> 01:03:29.340
a lot of bits or a lot
of components, which--

01:03:29.340 --> 01:03:32.760
when you do frequency
transform above 20khz,

01:03:32.760 --> 01:03:34.140
you don't need to store it.

01:03:34.140 --> 01:03:36.730
No human is ever
going to be hear it.

01:03:36.730 --> 01:03:38.250
And so far, we are
not compressing,

01:03:38.250 --> 01:03:40.380
I guess, audio for
dogs and animals.

01:03:40.380 --> 01:03:41.470
So we should be OK.

01:03:41.470 --> 01:03:41.970
It's for us.

01:03:41.970 --> 01:03:44.400
So that again gives
you a motivation.

01:03:44.400 --> 01:03:49.500
Even if you have some non-zero,
let's say, energy in components

01:03:49.500 --> 01:03:52.530
above, let's say 20khz,
you don't want to keep it.

01:03:52.530 --> 01:03:56.310
You will throw it because it
doesn't matter in practice.

01:03:56.310 --> 01:03:58.020
Your distortion,
really, the key idea

01:03:58.020 --> 01:04:01.980
there is, the key switch which
happens is your distortion.

01:04:01.980 --> 01:04:04.350
Mean square error is
not the right metric.

01:04:04.350 --> 01:04:07.200
At the end of the
day, in some sense,

01:04:07.200 --> 01:04:12.910
mean, human error is
the right metric there.

01:04:12.910 --> 01:04:15.950
And we are not going to talk
about this today at all.

01:04:15.950 --> 01:04:18.820
There's actually, in fact,
a whole lecture dedicated

01:04:18.820 --> 01:04:21.190
on this aspects because this
is not typically covered

01:04:21.190 --> 01:04:25.720
in a compression, but it's
very important for compression.

01:04:25.720 --> 01:04:29.180
But it's typically not covered
in information theory classes.

01:04:29.180 --> 01:04:32.620
So we'll talk more about
it after Thanksgiving.

01:04:32.620 --> 01:04:37.540
But yeah, there are really
two reasons working out.

01:04:37.540 --> 01:04:40.270
So one is more on the
KLT side, but the other

01:04:40.270 --> 01:04:42.770
is on the human side.

01:04:42.770 --> 01:04:46.800
And if you don't
remember anything else,

01:04:46.800 --> 01:04:49.050
this is the key
takeaway, compress

01:04:49.050 --> 01:04:50.670
the high-frequency
components more.

01:04:50.670 --> 01:04:51.720
You're going to be fine.

01:04:55.630 --> 01:04:58.900
And so DCT actually is one
of the, again, like I said,

01:04:58.900 --> 01:05:01.990
most popular transforms
in terms of compression.

01:05:01.990 --> 01:05:03.910
In compression world,
it's really popular.

01:05:03.910 --> 01:05:06.040
But again, depending
on your application,

01:05:06.040 --> 01:05:10.630
you might hear about DFT,
many other transforms.

01:05:10.630 --> 01:05:14.350
What DCT, is-- and we'll
look at some examples.

01:05:14.350 --> 01:05:17.290
Just right now let me just give
some summary if you have never

01:05:17.290 --> 01:05:19.180
heard about DCT before.

01:05:19.180 --> 01:05:22.630
It's basically discrete
cosine transform.

01:05:22.630 --> 01:05:25.420
So it's basically values
of cosine function

01:05:25.420 --> 01:05:26.650
at discrete points.

01:05:26.650 --> 01:05:28.930
It's just basically that.

01:05:28.930 --> 01:05:30.190
Again, it's linear.

01:05:30.190 --> 01:05:32.140
In fact, it is orthonormal.

01:05:32.140 --> 01:05:34.540
And again, it's just
a variant of DFT.

01:05:34.540 --> 01:05:36.640
So you can think of
the outputs of DCT

01:05:36.640 --> 01:05:38.952
as, again, doing some
frequency analysis.

01:05:38.952 --> 01:05:40.660
So you are just breaking
down your signal

01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:45.090
into different independent
frequency channels.

01:05:45.090 --> 01:05:46.375
It's a real-value transform.

01:05:46.375 --> 01:05:48.750
So this is where it's actually
different from the Fourier

01:05:48.750 --> 01:05:49.680
transform.

01:05:49.680 --> 01:05:51.750
FFT can lead to
imaginary values,

01:05:51.750 --> 01:05:53.970
but we don't want our
transform components

01:05:53.970 --> 01:05:56.790
to lead into imaginary
values, really.

01:05:56.790 --> 01:05:58.500
And so, yeah.

01:05:58.500 --> 01:06:01.650
So this is just another
property of DCT, which helps us.

01:06:01.650 --> 01:06:03.600
And finally, it's a
lossless transform.

01:06:03.600 --> 01:06:07.320
So again, we have the TT
inverse property working for us.

01:06:07.320 --> 01:06:11.720
So we can we can
just plug and play.

01:06:11.720 --> 01:06:13.835
And then various
versions of DCT exist.

01:06:13.835 --> 01:06:15.710
So if you actually go
look at the literature,

01:06:15.710 --> 01:06:19.730
you'll hear something about
DCT1, DCT2, DCT3, DCT4.

01:06:19.730 --> 01:06:20.540
It's OK.

01:06:20.540 --> 01:06:22.730
All of these are very--

01:06:22.730 --> 01:06:25.220
they basically differ in the
boundary conditions of how

01:06:25.220 --> 01:06:28.860
these matrices are derived.

01:06:28.860 --> 01:06:30.330
Yeah, don't worry about it.

01:06:30.330 --> 01:06:31.800
From the compression
angle, we'll

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:34.820
just think of DCT2,
which is the most--

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:37.320
so DCT2 is mostly
what's used in many

01:06:37.320 --> 01:06:39.510
of the practical
compressors and scenarios.

01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:42.690
So yeah.

01:06:42.690 --> 01:06:45.540
So again, let's build
some intuition for DCT2.

01:06:45.540 --> 01:06:48.600
And again, this is there
in the transform coding.

01:06:48.600 --> 01:06:49.950
It's reloading.

01:06:49.950 --> 01:06:52.200
This is again in the
transform coding notebook.

01:06:52.200 --> 01:06:54.630
So you can go home
and look at it.

01:06:54.630 --> 01:06:57.220
We'll put it up on the website.

01:06:57.220 --> 01:07:00.300
OK, so I've been handwavy.

01:07:00.300 --> 01:07:02.940
I never really defined DCT.

01:07:02.940 --> 01:07:05.520
I don't think that's the
point of this class again.

01:07:05.520 --> 01:07:09.270
But I have put it here, what
your DCT transforms look like.

01:07:09.270 --> 01:07:13.020
So if you want to go look, go at
home and look at it, please do.

01:07:13.020 --> 01:07:16.940
This will revise your
signal processing.

01:07:16.940 --> 01:07:18.650
So let's start, and
let's first look

01:07:18.650 --> 01:07:20.720
at the DCT transform matrix.

01:07:20.720 --> 01:07:24.440
So again, you have to
start with some block size.

01:07:24.440 --> 01:07:25.790
I can do this.

01:07:25.790 --> 01:07:26.570
Interesting.

01:07:26.570 --> 01:07:29.178
OK, so you have to
decide some block size

01:07:29.178 --> 01:07:30.470
over which you are going to do.

01:07:30.470 --> 01:07:33.020
So here I have decided
8 as the block size.

01:07:33.020 --> 01:07:36.380
So my DCT transform matrix is
going to be something like 8

01:07:36.380 --> 01:07:37.460
by 8.

01:07:37.460 --> 01:07:41.390
So I just literally wrote down
those formulae for the DCT

01:07:41.390 --> 01:07:44.960
transform, and I used that to
generate this 8 by 8 matrix.

01:07:44.960 --> 01:07:47.990
And the value in each
of them is the value

01:07:47.990 --> 01:07:51.380
which you are going to
multiply with that matrix.

01:07:51.380 --> 01:07:54.755
So what you see is that
the first row is basically

01:07:54.755 --> 01:07:56.880
a constant value.

01:07:56.880 --> 01:07:59.720
So if you were to multiply
this with a vector,

01:07:59.720 --> 01:08:04.110
the first component of the
transform will just be the--

01:08:04.110 --> 01:08:06.450
it's average, so as
to not exactly average

01:08:06.450 --> 01:08:11.310
some proportional to the
sum of all these components.

01:08:11.310 --> 01:08:15.210
And then, as you go down,
you see some colors changing,

01:08:15.210 --> 01:08:16.380
some wiggliness happening.

01:08:16.380 --> 01:08:18.960
So yellow here is
positive, blue is negative.

01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:21.000
So it's almost symmetric.

01:08:21.000 --> 01:08:27.870
But really, as I said, if you
think of each row of the matrix,

01:08:27.870 --> 01:08:30.960
that's basically
coming by discretizing

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:33.950
some cosine waveforms.

01:08:33.950 --> 01:08:38.979
So what I'm showing
here is, again--

01:08:38.979 --> 01:08:42.300
so this is row zero, so the
first row of our DCT matrix.

01:08:42.300 --> 01:08:45.210
And that's all some
constant value for the seven

01:08:45.210 --> 01:08:46.649
different components.

01:08:46.649 --> 01:08:48.510
Then, here this is row one.

01:08:48.510 --> 01:08:52.260
And now you see, this is like
discretized version of a half

01:08:52.260 --> 01:08:54.960
of cosine waveform something.

01:08:54.960 --> 01:08:57.029
The blue points are
the actual values,

01:08:57.029 --> 01:08:59.729
which exist in the transform
and so on and so forth.

01:08:59.729 --> 01:09:03.060
So you can see this is
increasing frequencies.

01:09:03.060 --> 01:09:05.399
The red is just the
continuous cosine curve.

01:09:05.399 --> 01:09:06.540
It's increasing frequency.

01:09:06.540 --> 01:09:09.819
And then you are basically
taking eight samples out of it.

01:09:09.819 --> 01:09:12.930
And that basically defines
the eight rows of DCT.

01:09:12.930 --> 01:09:15.180
So as you are
basically going down

01:09:15.180 --> 01:09:17.399
in terms of your
transform components,

01:09:17.399 --> 01:09:19.770
you are looking
at, in some sense,

01:09:19.770 --> 01:09:21.930
higher and higher frequency.

01:09:21.930 --> 01:09:24.569
Again, this is going to
be a hand-wavy thing.

01:09:24.569 --> 01:09:27.930
You can formalize all of
these intuitions and numbers.

01:09:27.930 --> 01:09:33.750
But this is just to give you
a quick sense of what DCT is.

01:09:33.750 --> 01:09:37.260
OK, so now, once you have
looked at the transform,

01:09:37.260 --> 01:09:38.880
we can actually
use this transform

01:09:38.880 --> 01:09:41.880
to do the forward transform
and the reverse transform.

01:09:41.880 --> 01:09:44.430
So these are just functions
which takes some signal.

01:09:44.430 --> 01:09:47.609
And again, here I've
just spelled it out.

01:09:47.609 --> 01:09:49.450
There are also SciPy
functions, et cetera,

01:09:49.450 --> 01:09:50.700
which you can use in practice.

01:09:50.700 --> 01:09:52.319
But this is for
pedagogical reasons.

01:09:52.319 --> 01:09:54.210
So you can just write
down these formally.

01:09:54.210 --> 01:09:56.370
Nothing fancy.

01:09:56.370 --> 01:09:59.940
OK, so now let's look
at some of the examples.

01:09:59.940 --> 01:10:04.290
What happens when you apply
DCT in various different cases?

01:10:04.290 --> 01:10:08.160
OK, so the first example is
that of a constant signal.

01:10:08.160 --> 01:10:11.700
So suppose you had a constant
signal with constant values,

01:10:11.700 --> 01:10:13.800
you can look at the
DCT and there you

01:10:13.800 --> 01:10:17.460
will see that, oh, it
peaks at the zero value.

01:10:17.460 --> 01:10:18.870
And that makes
sense because here

01:10:18.870 --> 01:10:20.520
you only have a
constant frequency.

01:10:20.520 --> 01:10:21.450
Nothing else.

01:10:21.450 --> 01:10:26.220
And then when you take the IDCT
of this transform components,

01:10:26.220 --> 01:10:31.640
DCT, you recover back the
original symbol signal in green.

01:10:31.640 --> 01:10:37.300
Yeah, everyone's following
what's happening here roughly?

01:10:37.300 --> 01:10:37.800
Yeah?

01:10:41.570 --> 01:10:46.220
OK, so now, in the next example,
let me just take some sinusoids

01:10:46.220 --> 01:10:49.740
with various varying frequency.

01:10:49.740 --> 01:10:52.290
And so this is now
my input signal.

01:10:52.290 --> 01:10:53.700
It has some frequency.

01:10:53.700 --> 01:10:55.710
I look at its DCT coefficient.

01:10:55.710 --> 01:10:58.980
It has value zero for most of
the high-frequency components,

01:10:58.980 --> 01:11:02.580
so higher values of my
DCT, but somehow some

01:11:02.580 --> 01:11:06.450
peaks at the index 2,
so component peaks.

01:11:06.450 --> 01:11:09.420
And then when you apply
the IDCT on this DCT,

01:11:09.420 --> 01:11:11.310
you recover back
the original symbol.

01:11:11.310 --> 01:11:14.160
And what do you see is on
the left, as I go down,

01:11:14.160 --> 01:11:18.350
I keep increasing the
frequency of my signals.

01:11:18.350 --> 01:11:20.900
And what you see is that
the DCT coefficients.

01:11:20.900 --> 01:11:26.180
Now, the energy in the DCT
coefficients is moving towards.

01:11:26.180 --> 01:11:28.940
So it's like-- again,
your DCT is tracking

01:11:28.940 --> 01:11:30.170
the frequency of the signals.

01:11:30.170 --> 01:11:32.120
In some sense, the
higher the value

01:11:32.120 --> 01:11:36.590
of the DCT component and
your transform side--

01:11:36.590 --> 01:11:38.930
sorry, the higher
the component, which

01:11:38.930 --> 01:11:41.300
is nonzero in the
transform side, that

01:11:41.300 --> 01:11:46.150
means higher the frequency
it has, the input signal has.

01:11:46.150 --> 01:11:48.280
OK, so again, it's
tracking frequencies

01:11:48.280 --> 01:11:49.480
is what I want to say.

01:11:49.480 --> 01:11:52.030
It's not really a delta
function because all of this

01:11:52.030 --> 01:11:54.610
depends on-- as soon as you
go from containers to discrete

01:11:54.610 --> 01:11:56.610
a lot of things starts
depending on the boundary

01:11:56.610 --> 01:11:58.030
conditions and exact samples.

01:11:58.030 --> 01:11:59.200
If you know about it, great.

01:11:59.200 --> 01:12:01.658
If you don't know about it,
don't worry about it right now.

01:12:04.270 --> 01:12:06.790
OK, so now let's look
at another example

01:12:06.790 --> 01:12:08.890
where we just have
a general signal.

01:12:08.890 --> 01:12:12.010
I just made some
triangle-like signal.

01:12:12.010 --> 01:12:14.980
I did it DCT.

01:12:14.980 --> 01:12:17.050
So again, it's 8 by 8.

01:12:17.050 --> 01:12:19.630
So I have seven
transform components.

01:12:19.630 --> 01:12:22.420
Now, for this signal, I see
it has more low-frequency

01:12:22.420 --> 01:12:24.710
component, but there is
some nonzero high-frequency

01:12:24.710 --> 01:12:25.210
component.

01:12:25.210 --> 01:12:27.700
For example, this
component at 5.

01:12:27.700 --> 01:12:29.180
You can't see my pencil, right?

01:12:29.180 --> 01:12:33.820
OK, so this component
at 5 is not really zero.

01:12:33.820 --> 01:12:34.810
There is a small stem.

01:12:34.810 --> 01:12:37.300
So there is some small
value, but it's mostly

01:12:37.300 --> 01:12:39.490
zero at the higher
frequency components.

01:12:39.490 --> 01:12:42.160
And again, when I do
the IDCT of this thing,

01:12:42.160 --> 01:12:44.710
you get back the same
signal, so far so good.

01:12:44.710 --> 01:12:46.690
But now you guys know
what we are going to do.

01:12:46.690 --> 01:12:48.370
So we are going to--
we are not going

01:12:48.370 --> 01:12:50.770
to keep the whole DCT when we
are doing the lossy compression.

01:12:50.770 --> 01:12:51.820
We are going to throw away.

01:12:51.820 --> 01:12:53.950
And the way we are going
to throw away is, again--

01:12:53.950 --> 01:12:55.420
let's just use our intuition.

01:12:55.420 --> 01:12:57.790
We don't have a theory
right now to help us.

01:12:57.790 --> 01:12:59.170
Let's just use our intuition.

01:12:59.170 --> 01:13:01.340
What will you do?

01:13:01.340 --> 01:13:04.310
You throw away the components
which have very low power.

01:13:04.310 --> 01:13:07.460
That's like one way to
compress and because then you

01:13:07.460 --> 01:13:10.220
don't have to worry about
a whole stream essentially

01:13:10.220 --> 01:13:13.040
of things to quantize, correct?

01:13:13.040 --> 01:13:17.070
And so, let's just do that
for this particular signal.

01:13:17.070 --> 01:13:22.090
And so what I'm going to do is
basically I'll have a threshold.

01:13:22.090 --> 01:13:24.910
And whatever components
lie below that threshold,

01:13:24.910 --> 01:13:27.400
I'm going to just
put it to zero.

01:13:27.400 --> 01:13:30.280
So the first one is threshold
zero, so I basically

01:13:30.280 --> 01:13:31.690
keep all the components.

01:13:31.690 --> 01:13:36.280
And you see that the
green reconstructed signal

01:13:36.280 --> 01:13:37.900
is matching exactly.

01:13:37.900 --> 01:13:40.930
But now I went ahead, I took
some very small threshold

01:13:40.930 --> 01:13:41.770
like 0.25.

01:13:41.770 --> 01:13:44.050
So everything in this
plus -0.25 window,

01:13:44.050 --> 01:13:47.230
I'm going to put it to zero.

01:13:47.230 --> 01:13:49.270
Actually, just this
guy goes to zero.

01:13:49.270 --> 01:13:50.253
So that was non-zero.

01:13:50.253 --> 01:13:50.920
Here, it's zero.

01:13:50.920 --> 01:13:52.510
You can't even see it.

01:13:52.510 --> 01:13:54.430
And you get this
recovered signal.

01:13:54.430 --> 01:13:56.860
So green is the
reconstructed signal.

01:13:56.860 --> 01:13:58.600
Red is the same original signal.

01:13:58.600 --> 01:14:01.900
So now you see, OK,
it won't match exactly

01:14:01.900 --> 01:14:04.960
because IDCT would match
exactly only if you

01:14:04.960 --> 01:14:06.160
have the same components.

01:14:06.160 --> 01:14:09.310
But now you see, you
introduce some error.

01:14:09.310 --> 01:14:12.240
OK, [AUDIO OUT]
and the experiment.

01:14:12.240 --> 01:14:15.840
Let's keep increasing
the threshold.

01:14:15.840 --> 01:14:17.370
Five.

01:14:17.370 --> 01:14:21.480
Now you see, at 0.5, all of
my high-frequency components

01:14:21.480 --> 01:14:22.740
have gone to zero.

01:14:22.740 --> 01:14:25.650
And I'm actually just
keeping three components

01:14:25.650 --> 01:14:28.390
instead of eight components.

01:14:28.390 --> 01:14:31.180
But still, my error seemed
like somewhat controlled.

01:14:31.180 --> 01:14:33.610
It's not really that
the green signal

01:14:33.610 --> 01:14:37.090
looks super different
from the less red right.

01:14:37.090 --> 01:14:38.950
Obviously, you are
introducing distortion,

01:14:38.950 --> 01:14:42.450
but it's not too much.

01:14:42.450 --> 01:14:45.030
Then you keep increasing,
keep truncating.

01:14:45.030 --> 01:14:48.510
At some point, you
basically keep just the--

01:14:48.510 --> 01:14:50.850
you keep nothing,
all the DC signal.

01:14:50.850 --> 01:14:54.150
At this point, obviously, your
errors are just over the roof.

01:14:54.150 --> 01:14:56.400
If you just keep the first
component, that's basically

01:14:56.400 --> 01:14:59.220
saying, oh, I'll just transmit
the average of these things.

01:14:59.220 --> 01:15:01.530
So that's not really useful.

01:15:01.530 --> 01:15:04.350
But maybe somewhere
here at threshold 2,

01:15:04.350 --> 01:15:06.450
or maybe at threshold
1 where you're keeping

01:15:06.450 --> 01:15:08.310
just two or three components.

01:15:08.310 --> 01:15:13.890
Depending on the scenario, these
errors might be more manageable.

01:15:13.890 --> 01:15:17.580
And so this is
really what DCT does.

01:15:17.580 --> 01:15:19.830
So you are splitting it
into transform components,

01:15:19.830 --> 01:15:22.560
and you are throwing
away maybe components.

01:15:22.560 --> 01:15:24.780
So in this example we
completely threw away.

01:15:24.780 --> 01:15:27.690
But again, like we discussed
in the previous example

01:15:27.690 --> 01:15:29.550
where rho was 0.5.

01:15:29.550 --> 01:15:31.650
Maybe you don't
completely throw away.

01:15:31.650 --> 01:15:34.710
Maybe you compress some
components more versus

01:15:34.710 --> 01:15:35.910
some other components less.

01:15:35.910 --> 01:15:38.640
And that's very common in a
lot of practical transforms.

01:15:42.300 --> 01:15:44.850
And this notebook is available.

01:15:44.850 --> 01:15:50.800
So you can go over it.

01:15:50.800 --> 01:15:54.360
Any questions?

01:15:54.360 --> 01:15:58.620
And after this, we are going
to just go five more minutes

01:15:58.620 --> 01:16:00.247
and have some fun.

01:16:00.247 --> 01:16:02.580
We are going to collect
everything which we have learned

01:16:02.580 --> 01:16:06.250
and make our first
lossy compressor.

01:16:06.250 --> 01:16:07.120
Yeah.

01:16:07.120 --> 01:16:10.440
How is this different
from Fourier transform?

01:16:10.440 --> 01:16:11.520
How is it different?

01:16:11.520 --> 01:16:15.388
You can think of it as
a sister-like transform.

01:16:15.388 --> 01:16:17.430
Basically, the boundary
conditions are different.

01:16:17.430 --> 01:16:21.000
So in Fourier transform,
here you assume symmetricity.

01:16:21.000 --> 01:16:26.332
And it's basically a different
transform, very similar

01:16:26.332 --> 01:16:27.290
like Fourier transform.

01:16:27.290 --> 01:16:31.720
But in Fourier transform,
unless you have--

01:16:31.720 --> 01:16:33.940
OK, I think the
real reason is it

01:16:33.940 --> 01:16:36.948
comes from the block
conditions, boundary conditions.

01:16:36.948 --> 01:16:38.740
So when you have these
boundary conditions,

01:16:38.740 --> 01:16:40.300
you want continuous signals.

01:16:40.300 --> 01:16:42.070
And in Fourier
transform, you can

01:16:42.070 --> 01:16:47.480
show that DCT accommodates
the boundary conditions better

01:16:47.480 --> 01:16:50.880
when you break down in blocks.

01:16:50.880 --> 01:16:53.197
Maybe I'm not able to
express it very clearly.

01:16:53.197 --> 01:16:54.780
I'll answer it better
after the class.

01:16:54.780 --> 01:16:55.800
You want to?

01:16:55.800 --> 01:16:57.300
It's like for
constant-type signals,

01:16:57.300 --> 01:16:59.440
I think DCT is more [INAUDIBLE].

01:17:02.800 --> 01:17:03.460
Yeah.

01:17:03.460 --> 01:17:05.410
Plus cosine is flat.

01:17:05.410 --> 01:17:06.190
Flat, yeah.

01:17:06.190 --> 01:17:09.210
But the sine [INAUDIBLE].

01:17:09.210 --> 01:17:12.790
So basically, cosine-- so sine
has a higher derivative at zero,

01:17:12.790 --> 01:17:13.290
actually.

01:17:13.290 --> 01:17:14.935
So in boundary
conditions-- so what

01:17:14.935 --> 01:17:17.310
happens is you have this
signal, but you are chunking it?

01:17:17.310 --> 01:17:19.650
So for example, in this
8 by 8, I won't really

01:17:19.650 --> 01:17:20.910
always get 8 signals.

01:17:20.910 --> 01:17:22.860
I'll have a continuous
stream of symbols.

01:17:22.860 --> 01:17:25.950
I'll chunk 8, then
chunk 8, then chunk 8.

01:17:25.950 --> 01:17:29.010
And so what you want is
when you do this and get

01:17:29.010 --> 01:17:31.470
the inverse transform you
want the boundaries shouldn't

01:17:31.470 --> 01:17:33.690
be like, oh-- there's
a sudden jump.

01:17:33.690 --> 01:17:37.890
Imagine you had first block
last element as value 1,

01:17:37.890 --> 01:17:40.770
and similarly again a bunch
of values one in the second

01:17:40.770 --> 01:17:42.240
block initial component.

01:17:42.240 --> 01:17:45.780
You want that constant to remain
constant across the blocks.

01:17:45.780 --> 01:17:48.960
And DCT, again, it's
a bit mathematical.

01:17:48.960 --> 01:17:50.040
I think I'll explain it.

01:17:50.040 --> 01:17:52.253
I'll pose this on on ED, a
much more detailed answer

01:17:52.253 --> 01:17:52.920
after the class.

01:17:52.920 --> 01:17:57.630
But DCT ensures that this
constant symbol signal remains

01:17:57.630 --> 01:17:59.820
constant when you throw
away compared to DFT

01:17:59.820 --> 01:18:02.220
which can lead to huge shifts.

01:18:02.220 --> 01:18:04.440
And so you can imagine
if you have huge shifts,

01:18:04.440 --> 01:18:07.440
and you have some
continuous signal,

01:18:07.440 --> 01:18:10.720
that might be very odd to see
even in lossy compression.

01:18:10.720 --> 01:18:12.820
That shows-- that's a very
common artifact called

01:18:12.820 --> 01:18:15.970
blocking artifacts because
you see things messing up

01:18:15.970 --> 01:18:20.740
at the boundaries.

01:18:20.740 --> 01:18:26.603
Let's maybe go a little ahead,
and I'll post much more detail.

01:18:26.603 --> 01:18:28.270
But for now, if you
don't know anything,

01:18:28.270 --> 01:18:30.400
you can think of
it as a sister of--

01:18:30.400 --> 01:18:33.400
sister transform of DFD.

01:18:33.400 --> 01:18:36.520
OK, so I'll just go
for five more minutes.

01:18:36.520 --> 01:18:40.040
I do want to do this.

01:18:40.040 --> 01:18:43.610
[INAUDIBLE] the pipeline for
a bare-bones practical lossy

01:18:43.610 --> 01:18:45.320
compression at this point.

01:18:45.320 --> 01:18:48.620
So we start with some
simple symbols Xn,

01:18:48.620 --> 01:18:51.170
you do some
transform, you get Yn.

01:18:51.170 --> 01:18:53.030
You quantize this.

01:18:53.030 --> 01:18:54.890
You get some codebook vectors.

01:18:54.890 --> 01:18:57.230
After that, you have
to do entropy coding.

01:18:57.230 --> 01:19:00.110
So you do lossless coding
of these codebook vectors,

01:19:00.110 --> 01:19:04.130
whatever you get, that
gives you the bit stream.

01:19:04.130 --> 01:19:06.740
At the end of the day, you
have to get to the bitstream,

01:19:06.740 --> 01:19:08.570
and this determines the rate.

01:19:08.570 --> 01:19:10.400
And then, on the
decoder side, you first

01:19:10.400 --> 01:19:11.900
do exact reverse steps.

01:19:11.900 --> 01:19:13.670
So you first do
entropy decoding.

01:19:13.670 --> 01:19:15.650
That gives you the
codebook indices,

01:19:15.650 --> 01:19:19.790
which you can use to decode
ties, which gives you some

01:19:19.790 --> 01:19:21.980
recovered transformed vectors.

01:19:21.980 --> 01:19:24.260
And finally, you do the
inverse transform to get back

01:19:24.260 --> 01:19:25.400
your original signal.

01:19:25.400 --> 01:19:27.950
And your distortion is
between what you started with

01:19:27.950 --> 01:19:29.600
and what you ended with.

01:19:29.600 --> 01:19:32.240
So this is like the
rate-distortion tradeoff.

01:19:32.240 --> 01:19:34.580
And the key point to note
is the only lossy step

01:19:34.580 --> 01:19:37.190
here is quantized.

01:19:37.190 --> 01:19:41.240
And this is really, I would
say this is like the bare bones

01:19:41.240 --> 01:19:42.245
practical at this point.

01:19:42.245 --> 01:19:44.120
You already have-- if
you do all these steps,

01:19:44.120 --> 01:19:46.520
you have a practical
lossy compressor.

01:19:46.520 --> 01:19:49.190
In fact, what we will
see in the next lecture,

01:19:49.190 --> 01:19:51.050
JPEG essentially does this.

01:19:51.050 --> 01:19:55.250
And it really optimizes
each block to its fullest,

01:19:55.250 --> 01:20:02.150
but it's essentially this exact
block diagram at its core.

01:20:02.150 --> 01:20:05.030
OK, and so we have lots
of design decisions.

01:20:05.030 --> 01:20:06.530
You can choose
different transforms.

01:20:06.530 --> 01:20:08.390
You can choose
different quantization.

01:20:08.390 --> 01:20:10.850
You can choose different
entropy coding schemes,

01:20:10.850 --> 01:20:13.610
and we have given you some
of these in the class.

01:20:13.610 --> 01:20:15.230
But again, something
to note, there

01:20:15.230 --> 01:20:18.230
are many, many other options
which we might not have covered.

01:20:18.230 --> 01:20:21.650
So we have already covered like
some options for each of these,

01:20:21.650 --> 01:20:26.325
so let's just go
ahead and look at one.

01:20:30.330 --> 01:20:32.070
So we're going to
go and look ahead

01:20:32.070 --> 01:20:34.260
at an audio compressor
which basically

01:20:34.260 --> 01:20:36.210
does this exact same thing.

01:20:36.210 --> 01:20:40.800
And this is thanks to Shubham
for having this notebook.

01:20:40.800 --> 01:20:46.863
So our audio compressor is going
to do exactly these steps which

01:20:46.863 --> 01:20:47.530
we talked about.

01:20:47.530 --> 01:20:49.210
Sorry.

01:20:49.210 --> 01:20:52.710
So we are going to do
transform, in our case, DCT.

01:20:52.710 --> 01:20:56.025
We are going to quantize using
just the scalar quantizer.

01:20:56.025 --> 01:20:57.900
And then we are going
to do-- we are actually

01:20:57.900 --> 01:21:02.730
not going to do entropy coding,
but we are going to look at it.

01:21:02.730 --> 01:21:07.540
So let's just look
at this notebook.

01:21:07.540 --> 01:21:10.380
OK, so this is just like
setting up some basic code.

01:21:10.380 --> 01:21:13.050
You want to be able to
quantize and quantize.

01:21:13.050 --> 01:21:15.330
You will do DCT, IDCT.

01:21:15.330 --> 01:21:18.270
You will do something like
low pass DCT, which is exactly

01:21:18.270 --> 01:21:21.150
the thing which we looked at,
in some sense thresholding

01:21:21.150 --> 01:21:24.443
the components and keeping
the others to zero.

01:21:24.443 --> 01:21:26.610
And then there are a bunch
of other helper function.

01:21:29.760 --> 01:21:31.340
And so, in a simple
audio compressor,

01:21:31.340 --> 01:21:33.770
you can actually play
with two components.

01:21:33.770 --> 01:21:36.320
You can play with the
frequency cutoff, which

01:21:36.320 --> 01:21:39.230
is like saying, OK, how many
components do I actually

01:21:39.230 --> 01:21:42.080
keep or you can play with the
number of quantization levels,

01:21:42.080 --> 01:21:43.700
and those will have
different effects.

01:21:43.700 --> 01:21:47.730
So this notebook has
lots of questions.

01:21:47.730 --> 01:21:50.870
So go again to attempt them.

01:21:50.870 --> 01:21:52.430
So let's just start.

01:21:52.430 --> 01:21:55.320
So I have already
loaded the data here.

01:21:55.320 --> 01:21:58.130
And so what you see here is
the transformation for one

01:21:58.130 --> 01:22:01.670
of the file on the x-axis is
like the frequency component.

01:22:01.670 --> 01:22:04.850
So you can think of these as
independent transform channels.

01:22:04.850 --> 01:22:06.770
And on the y-axis is the energy.

01:22:06.770 --> 01:22:08.870
And if you see for
this natural signal,

01:22:08.870 --> 01:22:10.850
there is a natural cliff.

01:22:10.850 --> 01:22:14.630
Maybe I should be
able to zoom out.

01:22:14.630 --> 01:22:17.250
OK, I'm not able
to zoom this out.

01:22:17.250 --> 01:22:21.440
But yeah, so you can see that
for this whatever audio signal,

01:22:21.440 --> 01:22:24.230
your energy suddenly drops
from this point onwards.

01:22:24.230 --> 01:22:26.870
So this was the point where
natural signals automatically

01:22:26.870 --> 01:22:29.540
have their statistics
such that there is

01:22:29.540 --> 01:22:31.340
low energy at higher frequency.

01:22:33.900 --> 01:22:36.060
And so we can do two things.

01:22:36.060 --> 01:22:40.530
We can either-- again, we
can either throw away--

01:22:40.530 --> 01:22:42.570
we'll decide to throw
away all the components

01:22:42.570 --> 01:22:44.670
above our frequency,
or we can decide

01:22:44.670 --> 01:22:48.540
to discretize each component
with more or less number

01:22:48.540 --> 01:22:50.550
of codebook vectors.

